# coff module

The `coff` module parses PE and COFF files for Windows targets.

## Constants

- `DOS_MAGIC`: `M`, `Z`.
- `PE_SIGNATURE`: `P`, `E`, 0, 0.
- `OptionalHeaderMagic.PE32`: 32-bit optional header.
- `OptionalHeaderMagic.PE32_PLUS`: 64-bit optional header.

## Types

### `Machine`

COFF machine type.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.
- `isKnown(raw)`: check if a raw machine value is known.

### `Subsystem`

PE subsystem.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `ComdatSelection`

COMDAT selection criteria.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SectionKind`

Classified section kind.

Values include code, initialized data, uninitialized data, and others.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SectionPermissions`

Mapped section permissions.

- `toChars(self)`: return a three-character permission string.

### `StorageClass`

COFF symbol storage class.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SpecialSection`

Special COFF section indices.

Constants: `UNDEFINED = 0`, `ABSOLUTE = -1`, `DEBUG = -2`.

### `DataDirectory`

PE data directory entry.

Fields: `virtual_address`, `size`.

### `OptionalHeader`

PE optional header.

Fields include magic, entry point, image base, subsystem, and data directories.

### `Relocation`

COFF relocation entry.

- `typeName(self, machine)`: return a readable type name.

### `SectionHeader`

COFF section header.

Methods:

- `isExecutable`, `isReadable`, `isWritable`: check permissions.
- `hasCode`, `hasInitializedData`, `hasUninitializedData`: check content type.
- `isComdat`, `isDiscardable`: check section traits.
- `hasRelocationOverflow`: check for overflow.
- `getAlignment`: return alignment.
- `getPermissions`: return `SectionPermissions`.
- `getNormalizedKind(self, resolved_name)`: classify a section.
- `getName(self, strtab_bytes)`: read the section name.
- `getBaseName(resolved_name)`: return the base section name.
- `getSortKey(resolved_name)`: return a sort key.
- `getData(self, file_bytes)`: return section bytes.
- `getRelocationCount(self, file_bytes)`: count relocations.
- `parseRelocation(self, file_bytes, index)`: parse one relocation.
- `parseAllRelocations(self, allocator, file_bytes)`: parse all relocations.

### `Symbol`

COFF symbol table entry.

Methods:

- `isUndefined`, `isCommon`, `isAbsolute`, `isExternal`, `isStatic`, `isFunction`.
- `getName(self, strtab_bytes)`: read the symbol name.

### `Header`

Parsed COFF or PE header.

Methods:

- `isExecutable`, `isDll`, `hasRelocsStripped`.
- `parse(bytes)`: parse the header.
- `parseSectionHeader(self, file_bytes, index)`: parse one section header.
- `parseAllSectionHeaders(self, allocator, file_bytes)`: parse all section headers.
- `getStringTable(self, file_bytes)`: return the string table.
- `parseSymbol(self, file_bytes, index)`: parse one symbol.
- `parseAllSymbols(self, allocator, file_bytes)`: parse all symbols.

## Constants groups

- `characteristics`: COFF file characteristic flags.
- `section_flags`: COFF section characteristic flags.

## File characteristic constants

`sibl.coff_characteristics` contains COFF file characteristic flags. These include `RELOCS_STRIPPED`, `EXECUTABLE_IMAGE`, `LINE_NUMS_STRIPPED`, `LOCAL_SYMS_STRIPPED`, `LARGE_ADDRESS_AWARE`, `BIT32_MACHINE`, `DEBUG_STRIPPED`, `DLL`, and others defined in `src/coff.zig`.

## Section flag constants

`sibl.coff_section_flags` contains COFF section characteristic flags. These include `CNT_CODE`, `CNT_INITIALIZED_DATA`, `CNT_UNINITIALIZED_DATA`, `MEM_EXECUTE`, `MEM_READ`, `MEM_WRITE`, `LNK_COMDAT`, `LNK_REMOVE`, `LNK_NRELOC_OVFL`, and others defined in `src/coff.zig`.

## Error sets

- `Error`
- `SectionError`
- `SymbolError`
- `RelocationError`

## Example

```zig
const coff_hdr = try sibl.coff.Header.parse(mapped.data);
const sections = try coff_hdr.parseAllSectionHeaders(allocator, mapped.data);

for (sections) |sh| {
    const name = try sh.getName(strtab_bytes);
    std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{name});
}
```

## Notes

- Use `Header.isExecutable` and `Header.isDll` for PE files.
- Section names can come from the string table when they start with `/`.
- Relocation counts can use overflow tables.
- The parser keeps slices into the mapped file.
